  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Integrating Movement Ecology into Law Enforcement Strategies to Protect Forest Elephants in Gabonese Parks</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this two-year project is to conserve forest elephants in the Wonga Wongu Presidential Reserve and the Minkb-Ivindo complex. This project seeks to reduce poaching of elephants for ivory by guiding law enforcement with rigorous information on forest elephant movements, habitat use, and diets. Specific activities include: (1) data collection on elephant locations, diet, human activity in elephant habitats, and population demography to determine elephant habitat use and needs in the focal landscapes; (2) modeling of elephant home ranges, resource selection functions, and habitat connectivity to evaluate the efficacy of corridors that would better connect elephant habitat and protected areas; and (3) provisioning data, real-time location maps, and analytic tools to protected area managers and analyzing and interpreting spatial models to integrate elephant movement ecology into law enforcement strategies.</narrative>
  </description>
